## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about multi-cycle 3-valued constraint propagation, a common motif in policy engines and configuration management where Unknowns must be carefully propagated to avoid false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F ordering, ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Implication as a→b = ¬a ∨ b.
- Temporal constraints: handling two cycles and cross-time implications.
- Backtracking propagation: prune values that would force a constraint to F.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function litOr(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function litAnd(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return litOr(na, b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to N predicates and K cycles while preserving polynomial-time propagation?
- Compare Kleene vs Priest-style three-valued logics for the same constraint set in terms of guaranteed satisfiability detection.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to translate graph-based access policy constraints into a three-valued logical solver while managing constraint satisfaction risks. The directed graph structure introduces transitive reasoning requirements, making the problem more realistic than handling independent logical clauses.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation using Kleene semantics (T > U > F)
- Arc-consistency enforcement on directed constraint graphs
- Not-both-true constraint handling and conflict detection
- Backtracking strategies for partial truth assignments

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vals, edges) {
  // vals: {N1:'T'|'F'|'U',...}
  // edges: [[from, to], ...]
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'F') {
        return false; // Constraint violated
      }
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'U') {
        vals[to] = 'T';
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason with 3-valued semantics under realistic, constraint-based settings and design a compact propagation+backtracking strategy.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic: {T, F, U}
- A→B as ¬A ∨ B with ¬ and ∨ defined per Kleene semantics
- Constraint propagation and depth-limited backtracking
- Practical mapping to sensor fusion/telemetry validation in fleets

## Code Example

```javascript
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function or(a,b){ return (order[a] >= order[b]) ? a : b; }
function and(a,b){ return (order[a] <= order[b]) ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }
```

